<SiteLayout title="Image Quality and Compression Guide | ConvertAnyFile" description="Understand lossy vs lossless compression, quality sliders, generation loss, resizing, and how to create practical image delivery copies.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><span>/</span><span>Image quality</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Compression basics</span><h1>Smaller is useful only when the image still works.</h1><p>Compression is a tradeoff between bytes, visual quality, compatibility, and the job the image needs to perform.</p></div></section>
<section class="article-shell"><article class="site-container article"><h2>Lossy and lossless are different promises</h2><p>Lossless formats preserve the represented pixel data through encoding and decoding. Lossy formats intentionally discard or approximate information to reduce file size. Neither category is automatically better; they solve different delivery problems.</p><h2>Quality sliders are encoder settings</h2><p>A quality value is not a universal measurement. Different codecs and implementations map the same number to different compression behavior. A value of 80 in JPEG should not be treated as directly equivalent to 80 in WebP or AVIF.</p><h2>Generation loss matters</h2><p>If you repeatedly decode and re-encode a lossy image, each generation can introduce additional artifacts. For important source material, keep a master and create new delivery copies from that master rather than endlessly converting previous delivery copies.</p><h2>Resize before over-optimizing compression</h2><p>Pixel dimensions are often the biggest factor in image weight. If an image will never be displayed wider than 1200 pixels, shipping a 6000-pixel source wastes decoding memory and bandwidth regardless of the file extension.</p><h2>A tiny file is not always a successful result</h2><p>Compression can make a file impressively small while destroying text edges, skin texture, gradients, or fine product detail. Evaluate the image at the size people will actually see it.</p><h2>Conversion cannot restore missing information</h2><p>Saving a compressed JPG as PNG stops the new file from using JPEG compression, but it does not reconstruct detail already removed by the original JPG encode.</p><div class="callout"><strong>A practical workflow</strong><p>Keep a good source master. Resize for the destination. Choose the format based on compatibility and image content. Then tune quality until the delivery copy is small enough without obvious visual damage.</p></div></article></section>
</SiteLayout>
